{:edb/db-before-t 1387 :edb/db-after-t 1388 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534700 50 #inst "2026-10-12T08:15:25.648Z" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186061386 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433287 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186061386 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433287 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186063935 1071 "What is the value of $0 - 4$?" 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186063935 1071 "What is the value of $0 - 4?$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186063935 1074 "To subtract $4$ from $0$, we start at $0$ on the number line and move $4$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/0b/0b52e781b6de8144eef5248de1941ea102834b77ec9ec07a9c266fd395b2b0e8.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-4$. Therefore, $0-4 = -4$." 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186063935 1074 "To subtract $4$ from $0,$ we start at $0$ on the number line and move $4$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/0b/0b52e781b6de8144eef5248de1941ea102834b77ec9ec07a9c266fd395b2b0e8.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-4.$ Therefore, $0-4 = -4.$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186063936 1071 "Find the value of $170 - 281$." 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186063936 1071 "Find the value of $170 - 281.$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186063936 1074 "Since $281$ is larger than $170$, the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n$281 - 170$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n281-170 = 111\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n170 - 281 = -111\n$$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186063936 1074 "Since $281$ is larger than $170,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n281 - 170\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/32/32ceb366008206a2d765e3507a6b1d1d60682c407e9eb26ea90d230159a0377a.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n281-170 = 111.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n170 - 281 = -111.\n$$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433314 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433317 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433318 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433319 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433320 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433315 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433316 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433321 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433322 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064418 1050 420.0 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064526 1071 "$12 - 36 =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186064526 1071 "$12-36=${{field-1}}" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186064526 1074 "Since $36$ is larger than $12,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n36-12\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n36-12=24.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n12-36=-24.\n$$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186065412 1064 "When we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n3 - 5\n$$\n\nTo subtract $5$ from $3$, we start at $3$ on the number line and move $5$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/57/57ac1fe7a28ae5fefadf900d2276d499cdccf01df48b819b0aafa5347a2ec506.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3-5 = -2\n$$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186065412 1064 "When we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n3 - 5\n$$\n\nTo subtract $5$ from $3,$ we start at $3$ on the number line and move $5$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/57/57ac1fe7a28ae5fefadf900d2276d499cdccf01df48b819b0aafa5347a2ec506.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n3-5 = -2.\n$$" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186065413 1064 "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers.\n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37$, the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n${\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}$.\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31\n$$\n\nAnd that's it!\n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers." 13194139534700 17592186045417 false] [17592186065413 1064 "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers. \n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37,$ the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/db/db192e5c1f089e3d4cc2a7b1238392e44a7ba36bff99345a997042c16ebc304f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! \n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers." 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433283 1080 "312" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433283 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433283 1083 #uuid "13042cd9-3fe1-5a6e-9f68-9d984f61c24d" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433284 1080 "-312" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433284 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433284 1083 #uuid "1770c383-61fb-5018-b6c5-fdbcc32171fc" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433285 1080 "0" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433285 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433285 1083 #uuid "1c685f83-613d-5efb-af1b-74efe04a9fc8" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433286 1080 "-3" 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [17592186433286 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534700 17592186045417 true] [175921